I've also added comments for clarity.

---

### Helper Methods

#### 1. `FactorialOrSum`
Computes either the factorial of `n` (if it's less than the sum of 1..n) or the sum of 1..n.

```csharp
using System;

public static int FactorialOrSum(int n)
{
    if (n == 0)
        return 0;
    if (n == 1)
        return 1;

    long factorial = 1;
    int sum = 0;
    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++)
    {
        factorial *= i;
        sum += i;
    }

    // Use long to avoid overflow for large n
    if (factorial < sum)
        return (int)factorial;
    else
        return sum;
}
```
> **Note:** For large `n`, the factorial will overflow quickly. You may want to use `Big integers` (like `System.Numerics.BigInteger`) for larger inputs, but for most small `n` this is fine.

---

#### 2. `Fibonacci`
Classic iterative Fibonacci.

```csharp
public static int Fibonacci(int n)
{
    if (n == 0)
        return 0;
    if (n == 1)
        return 1;

    var a = 0;
    var b = 1;
    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++)
    {
        var temp = a;
        a = b;
        b = temp + b;
    }
    return b;
}
```

---

#### 3. `f(n, m)` – Returns a list of tuples (or `null` for padding)

In C#, you can use `Tuple<int, int?>` to allow `null` for the second element (like Python's `None`).  
Alternatively, you could use a custom struct or a class, but for brevity, I'll use `Tuple<int, int?>`.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public static List<Tuple<int, int?>> F(int n, int m)
{
    var results = new List<Tuple<int, int?>>();

    // First part: from 1 to n
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
    {
        int value = FactorialOrSum(i);
        int fib = Fibonacci(i);
        results.Add(new Tuple<int, int?>(value, fib));
    }

    // Second part: from n+1 to m-1 (inclusive), pad with null
    if (m > n)
    {
        for (int i = n + 1; i < m; i++)
        {
            results.Add(new Tuple<int, int?>(default, default)); // (0, null)
        }
    }

    return results;
}
```

