response:
You **can call a managed (`.NET) custom action from an MSI (Windows Installer) package directly**, without needing an unmanaged intermediary like P/Invoke or native DLLs. This is possible using the **MSI Custom Action feature with the `CustomAction` attribute** and by using **C# code compiled as a .NET assembly**, exposed via **COM interop** or by using **P/Invoke from managed code** (via `System.Runtime.InteropServices`). However, the most straightforward and "purely managed" way is to use **COM interop** so your managed DLL can be registered as a COM object and consumed by the MSI.

Here's how you can do it step by step:

---

## **Option 1: Expose Your Managed DLL as a COM Object**

### **A. Build Your Managed DLL as a COM Callable Wrapper**

1. **Add Reference to Your Managed DLL in a New Console App Project**
   - In Visual Studio, create a new Console Application project.
   - Add a reference to your managed DLL (e.g., `MyCustomAction.dll`).
   - In the new project, add a class that **implements `IUnknown` and `IDispatch` interfaces** (for COM interop).

   ```csharp
   using System.Runtime.InteropServices;
   using System.Runtime.InteropServices.ComTypes;

   [ComVisible(true)]
   [Guid("YOUR_GUID_HERE")]
   public interface IMyCustomAction
   {
       string DoSomething(string param);
   }

   [ClassInterface(ClassInterfaceType.None)]
   [ComDefaultInterface]
   public class MyCustomActionCOM : IMyCustomAction
   {
       private Type _myDLLType;
       [DllImport("MyCustomAction.dll", SetLastError = true)]
       static extern IntPtr GetModuleHandle(string moduleName);

       [DllImport("kernel32.dll", SetLastError = true)]
       static extern IntPtr LoadLibrary(string lpModuleName);

       [DllImport("kernel32.dll", SetLastError = true)]
       static extern bool FreeLibrary(IntPtr hModule);

       [DllImport("Coapi.dll", SetLastError = true)]
       static extern IntPtr CoCreateInstance([In] Type interfaceType, [In] object interfaceInstance, [In] IntPtr VariantAllocContext, [Out] IntPtr pInterface);

       [DllImport("Coapi.dll", SetLastError = true)]
       static extern bool CoDestroyInstance(IntPtr oInterface);

       private IntPtr _handle;

       public string DoSomething(string param)
       {
           // Get the handle to your managed DLL
           IntPtr hMod = GetModuleHandle("MyCustomAction.dll");
           if (hMod == IntPtr.Zero)
               throw new Exception("Could not load managed DLL as COM object");

           // Load the assembly
           IntPtr hAssembly = LoadLibrary(hMod.ToString());

           // Get the type from the assembly
           Type type = Type.GetTypeFromCLSID(new Guid("YOUR_GUID_HERE")); // Use the same GUID as your COM interface
           if (type == null)
               throw new Exception("Type not found in assembly");

           // Create an instance of your managed class implementing the COM interface
           object instance = Activator.CreateInstance(type);

           // Cast to your COM interface
           IMyCustomAction comObj = (IMyCustomAction)instance;

           // Call your method
           return comObj.DoSomething(param);
       }
   }
   ```

   > **Note:** You must set up a **GUID** for your COM interface and make sure your class implements the required COM interfaces (`IDispatch`, `IUnknown`).  
   > You may need to use [C++/CLI](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/interop/how-to-expose-managed-code-as-a-com-object) or [C# with manual COM registration](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/framework/com/using-c-sharp-with-windows-installer) for more complex scenarios.

2. **Build the Wrapper as a Native DLL**
   - Compile the above C# code as a **native DLL** (not a managed assembly).  
   - This DLL will expose your managed code as a COM object.

3. **Register the COM Object**
   - Use the **RegAsm.exe** tool to register your DLL as a COM server:
     ```cmd
     regasm MyCustomAction.dll /codebase /tlb /id MyCustomAction
     ```
   - This will create a type library (`MyCustomAction.tlb`) and register the CLSID.

---

### **B. Use the COM Object in an MSI Custom Action**

In your **MSI package**, you can define a custom action that uses the COM object like this:

```xml
<CustomActions>
    <CustomAction
        Id="RunMyManagedAction"
        Title="Run My Managed Action"
        ProgID="MyCompany.MyApp.MyCustomAction"
        ClassID="{GUID_OF_YOUR_COM_CLASS}"
        Description="Runs a managed action."
        RunOrder="1000"
    >
        <Code Language="csharp">
            using System;
            using System.Runtime.InteropServices;
            using System.Windows.Forms;
            // Import the necessary COM types
            [DllImport("shell32.dll", SetLastError = true)]
            static extern IntPtr CreateWindowEx(
                uint dwStyle,
                string lpClassName,
                string lpWindowName,
                int nCmdShow,
                IntPtr hParent,
                IntPtr lpMaxSize,
                IntPtr lpMinSize,
                IntPtr lpStyle,
                IntPtr lpParent,
                IntPtr lpWindowPos,
                uint dwMaxCount,
                IntPtr lpvParams,
                uint dwWinPos,
                out IntPtr lpvResult);

            [DllImport("shell32.dll", SetLastError = true)]
            static extern bool PostMessage(IntPtr hWnd, uint Msg, IntPtr wParam, IntPtr lParam);

            // ... Add code to activate a window or use the COM object here

            // Example: Activate the COM object's window (if you exposed a Form or UI)
            IntPtr hComObj = CoCreateInstance(...); // Use your wrapper's GUID
            // ... get handle to the COM object's window
            IntPtr hWnd = GetWindowHandleFromCOM(hComObj);
            if (hWnd != IntPtr.Zero)
            {
                PostMessage(hWnd, WM_SYSCOMMAND, (IntPtr)0x0100, IntPtr.Zero); // Activate
            }

            // Or call the COM method directly:
            // string result = Marshal.InvokeMethod(hComObj, ...); // More complex with marshalling

            // For simplicity, you can just show a message box from the COM object
            // e.g., call a method that shows a MessageBox via the wrapper
        </Code>
    </CustomAction>
</CustomActions>
```

> **Note:** Directly calling managed methods from unmanaged code (the MSI execution context) is not possible without marshalling. Using COM is the standard bridge.

---

## **Option 2: P/Invoke from Managed Code (Not Recommended for MSI)**

You **could** write a P/Invoke signature in your managed DLL and call it from the MSI using `PInvoke` in C#. However, the MSI itself is unmanaged, so you'd need to:

- Expose a public static method in your managed DLL with a `[DllImport]` signature.
- Use `System.Runtime.InteropServices.PInvoke` in your MSI custom action code (C# or native C++).

But this is **less clean**, and you lose the benefit of true managed code safety and design. It's generally better to use **COM** for this purpose.

---

## **Option 3: Use Custom Action with .NET 6+ Native Code (Not Managed)**

If you want to avoid both unmanaged DLLs and COM, you could write the custom action in **native C++** using the Windows Installer SDK, but that's not what you asked for.

---

## **Summary Table**

| Approach | Managed Code in DLL | Unmanaged Intermediary Needed? | Complexity | Recommended? |
|----------|---------------------|-------------------------------|------------|--------------|
| COM Wrapper | ✅ | ❌ (uses managed code + COM interop) | Medium | ✅ Yes |
| P/Invoke from Managed DLL | ❌ (only P/Invoke entry points) | ❌ (but you must write P/Invoke signatures in managed code) | Medium | ⚠️ Not ideal |
| Native C++ Custom Action | ❌ | ❌ | Low | Only if you don't want managed code |

---

## **Best Practice Recommendation**

**Expose your managed code as a COM object via a managed C# COM Callable Wrapper, register it, and call it from your MSI using COM interop.**  
This is the standard, supported, and cleanest way to integrate managed code into a Windows Installer package.

---

## **References & Tools**

- [Microsoft Docs: Exposing Managed Code as a COM Object](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/framework/com/using-c-sharp-with-windows-installer)
- [How to Register a Managed DLL as COM](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/framework/com/using-c-sharp-with-windows-installer#registering-a-managed-class-as-a-com-object)
- [MSI Custom Actions Reference](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/msi/msi-custom-actions)

